# e_ncode and d_ecode are APL's encode and decode (B12), radix on the
# left. e_ncode gives the digits of a number in a mixed radix, the
# last radix the least significant; a 0 radix takes all that is left.
# Its shape is the radix's shape then the right argument's, so a
# vector gives one column per item. d_ecode is the inverse: a scalar
# radix extends, and a matrix gives one number per column.
== SOURCE
2 2 2 e_ncode 5
2 2 2 e_ncode 5 6
24 60 60 e_ncode 3725
0 10 e_ncode 123
10 e_ncode 123
2 2 e_ncode 7
2 2 2 e_ncode -1
2 d_ecode 1 0 1
10 d_ecode 1 2 3
24 60 60 d_ecode 1 2 5
2 d_ecode 3 2 r_eshape 1 1 0 1 1 0
2 d_ecode 2 2 2 e_ncode r_ange 7
2 d_ecode 1 2 3 = 1
